 ## Overview
-Resolution Adaptation adjusts the spatial discretization of the rendering process—either the output image resolution, the underlying voxel grid resolution, or the sampling density—to balance visual fidelity against computational cost. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Adaptive Refinement for Volume Rendering](1892-adaptive-refinement-for-volume-rendering.md) (which focuses on sample count) and [Ray-Centric Adaptive Step and Termination](5938-ray-centric-adaptive-step-and-termination.md) (which focuses on integration step size) by primarily targeting the spatial granularity of the data or the final pixel buffer.
+Resolution Adaptation refers to techniques that vary the spatial discretization of the rendering output or the underlying data representation to balance visual fidelity against computational cost [S1][S2, unofficial][S3]. It solves the problem of performance instability in volume rendering, where high-frequency details or large datasets cause frame rate dips or excessive memory usage [S1][S2, unofficial][S3]. Unlike [Adaptive Sampling and Resolution](1412-adaptive-sampling-and-resolution.md), which focuses on sample count and step size along rays, Resolution Adaptation specifically targets the spatial granularity of the rendered image or the voxel grid structure itself [S1][S2, unofficial][S3].
 
